{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh","id":1547490,"title":"Am I missing something about the Sluagh?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-01-03T10:07:27-08:00","id":1547490,"title":"Am I missing something about the Sluagh?","content":"I'm trying to create a Sluagh NPC and it seems like the kith are very difficult to fit into a role - other than creepy store owner, sewer-dwelling loner, or slinky goth.<br /> <br /> Moreover it feels like their birthrights just don't do anything useful - after all what's the point of being able to see/speak to spirits but have no other ability to affect them? There aren't even any arts that would work on ghosts, unless I'm mistaken?<br /> <br /> So what's the deal with Sluagh? Are they just one of the authors collection of spare parts to fill a space or do they have some meaning within the whole?<br /> <br /> I'm at a loss, please help.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-03T11:02:01-08:00","id":1547491,"title":"","content":"Hacker or generally online type is also pretty common.<br /> <br /> And most ghosts can't affect the living world, while any you would see are going to have things in the living world they would want to protect, called Fetters. This means that the sluagh can be even better at spying and information gathering than you might think, because they can form alliances with ghosts to do spying for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10243-zenten","name":"zenten","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10243_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"488"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-03T11:38:54-08:00","id":1547494,"title":"","content":"The Sluagh do suffer a bit from the 1e of the WoD tending to do cool concepts first, and function character types for group play second. Though IME people tend to be more frustrated by how much the books spend on them being antisocial than their Birthrights (as both are very useful to the kinds of concepts people associate with Sluagh broadly).<br /> <br /> When it comes to ghosts/wraiths: first who even needs Arts when the vast majority of changelings can't sense the dead at all? Finding a ghost with enough intelligence and rationality to make deals with means the Sluagh can offer their services to protect Fetters in exchange for favors such as spying in places the Sluagh might not want to do to preparations for fae intrusion.<br /> <br /> There's also nothing stopping Arts broadly from working on the dead. Wraiths, like all Prodigals, fall under the Actor Realm (which means befriending them and getting to know the the ones haunting your local area makes working powers on them even easier). Though many physical Arts don't work on ghosts due to the whole inability to touch them, Arts like Contract and Naming are completely functional on the dead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-03T15:46:52-08:00","id":1547502,"title":"","content":"Something that helps me when I think about the kith (or any splat in general tbh) is to try to find some archetypal or thematic core to them that I can chew on and play with (along with some signature concepts and myriad tid-bits). Now YMMV depending on the tone and play style you go for but here\u2019s some ideas I try to play around with.<br /> <br /> IMO both the Slaugh &amp; Redcaps are among the trickiest core kiths to nail down because at first blush you have to almost ask yourself: \u2018why aren\u2019t these guys thallain?\u2019. After all, they\u2019re described as often drawing upon their glamour from fear and horror, which certainly resembles the behavior of the Children of Nightmare. Now not everyone is satisfied with the explanations about how as kithain they rely upon the \u2018safer\u2019 forms of terror and fright, at least compared to the thallain, but YMMV.<br /> <br /> So the Slaugh are the spookiest of the core kiths. They\u2019re gothic spirits who like to hang out in the sets from a Tim Burton movie (the sewers from Batman, the scientist\u2019s manor from Edward Scissorhands, etc.) as well as places like Arkham Asylum, the House of Usher, Lou Cipher\u2019s pawn shop, etc. They live on classic gothic trappings: abandoned (probably haunted) houses, falling leaves, mildew and cobwebs, antiques. They\u2019re often described as wet and moist, almost resembling humanoid eels (I find this description of wet, cool, and moist interesting as it opposed a possible description of dry and dusty; it implies that the Slaugh are still filled with life and vital energy, albeit the life of crawling things and teeming ooze). <br /> <br /> Their dedication to being the gothic kith is also demonstrated in their tendency towards being antiquarians. The gothic is fascinated with the old and abandoned, often luxuriating in the idea that the past is never really past and that the ghosts we think we\u2019ve buried still haunt us, whispering secrets just out of the range of our hearing. The original gothics, as Romantics, often chafed under contemporary ideas of enlightenment rationality and advances of science which had banished the enchantment and magic of the old world as \u2018dusty superstition\u2019. It\u2019s no coincidence that so much gothic fiction showcases the undead old worlds of mysterious sorcery returning to plague the moderns who cannot comprehend them. This same infatuation with old things and harkening to buried traditions is reflected in the goth subculture movement and it\u2019s ironic fascinating with Victorian aesthetics, a fascination explicitly shared by the Slaugh. <br /> <br /> These things described above as well as a few other tid bits are what I try to use as my basis of inspiration for the Slaugh. Some of those miscellaneous tidbits including: the original Slaugh kith book describing how albeit Slaugh often being boogie men used to frighten children into obedience, proper Slaugh consider actual harm against children to be anathema (unlike Thallains) and only ever participate in sinister theatrics to frighten children back into the correct behavior (one could make an interesting story about how the Slaugh themselves do not necessarily control what \u2018correct behavior\u2019 is and that perhaps some Slaugh may feel conflicted about the social norms they are obliged to enforce upon the unruly children they are supposed to scare into good behavior), as is their prescribed role in the stories told by parents and elders; also Slaugh\u2019s \u2018Squirm\u2019 birthright makes them capable of getting into just about anything-anywhere, which makes an interesting aspect of the Slaugh their capacity to show up where you\u2019d least expect and be virtually impossible to keep out (as befits a kith obliged at times to slide under window sills to hide under beds or crawl through holes in floorboards in order to lurk in closets); the last little tidbit I like is that I always try to have at least one Slaugh in my setting whose a Cryptkeeper type (can\u2019t let the Nosferatu have all the fun). <br /> <br /> So taking this all together, where do we go from here? Well here are some ideas: Slaugh\u2019s fascination with old things leads them to naturally learn the history of places and things from the ghosts that linger there <i>(do the Slaugh attempt to help the restless dead solve their unfinished business or do they refuse for fear of losing their spectral friends and haunted playhouse? how do the Slaugh deal with ghost\u2019s who have values and desires that are incompatible with the morality of the present day living? What other forms of antiquing Slaugh can we have other than the classical Neo-Victorian? Are there Antebellum Slaugh? Turn of the Century Slaugh that sleep under the hoods of old Ford T-1s? Neo-Roman Slaugh who haunt ruins in tattered togas and recite funeral dirges in Latin?)</i>, they show how festering with life abandoned places can be <i>(do Slaugh try to lure humans into abandoned places? How does a Slaugh feel about someone attempting to renovate an old ruined house? Do they permit human life to return to the place or do they seek to defend the forms of life which human habitation which necessarily destroy - the birthright of mold, spiders, &amp; rats? What if the Yuppie owners want to convert a distinguished regency home into chic modernist apartments?)</i>, they play the role of boogies for human children <i>(see above for potential conflicts in this role)</i>, they naturally become reservoirs of lost knowledge both mundane and arcane <i>(do they share it with fellow changelings? can a sidhe squire trying to woo her lord-love really be trusted with the ingredients of the invisibility potion needed to sneak past the chaperones?)</i>, they are capable of getting into just about anywhere and doing it quietly <i>(under what circumstances would they agree to work as spies, thieves, or assassins? and, for that matter, under whose employ?)</i>, and so on.<br /> <br /> This has been somewhat rambling but I hope that there are interesting or useful ideas somewhere in there!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-04T10:01:46-08:00","id":1547517,"title":"","content":"One of my dislikes with Changeling: the Dreaming is that it is not always easy to connect a Kith with the kind of fairies they are supposed to emulate. Some are easy. Boggans relate to any kind of household or domestic fairies of which there are many examples. Nockers are obviously fairies who live underground, mine, and create magical items. Other kiths are either too specific or too vague. Sluagh are among the later.<br /> <br /> Yes, sluagh are obviously inspired by the Scottish fairy of the same name, but they are the host of the unforgiven dead which is not the same thing as the kith. Instead, the kith just seems to be a generic &quot;spooky fairy&quot; type. However, there don't seem to be many such fairies, and those that are scary often fit one of the other kiths better. Despite my attempts to read more fairy tales and folklore, I can't find any examples that make me go, &quot;Ah, that should be a sluagh!&quot; Instead, at best they just fill the role of general childhood boogeymen that are scary, but not very harmful. <br /> <br /> Folklore does have many examples of fairy type creatures whose purpose seems to be to keep children away from dangerous places until they become old enough to safely handle those places/situations. Or to stop being nuisances that bother their parents or other adults. But again, many actual specific fairies of this kind seem to fit other kiths better. So we're faced with the most generic type of nursery bogie. Still if we go down that route, we have another opportunity to use that theme to make them a kind of protectors of children whether they be kind neighbors, store owners of products that children like (maybe candy or toys), babysitters, governesses, school teachers and the like. Whether the sluagh kith is well designed for that is another issue.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh#post1547490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Moreover it feels like their birthrights just don't do anything useful - after all what's the point of being able to see/speak to spirits but have no other ability to affect them? There aren't even any arts that would work on ghosts, unless I'm mistaken?</div> </div> </div> </div>While they cannot control or threaten ghosts since they don't have powers, they do have something else they can do which does not require special powers to affect the dead. They can befriend ghosts and do favors for them. In a sense, they are just very good mediums that can do that &quot;job&quot; much better than most human mediums. So they can protect fetters, assist them in hunting Pathos, and do other favors for them in the Skinlands that they cannot do themselves. In exchange, the ghosts can do them equivalent favors like spying on people, using the occasional Arcanoi to accomplish something for them, and other favors.<br /> <br /> Of course, that means the ST needs to work a lot on developing who these ghosts are, what they want, and what they can do. Some STs are always up for that challenge, while for others it is a difficulty so they neglect it, and PCs of that kith can never make good use of their birthright. But if the ST is up for it, then the sluagh just need to be friendly and kind to ghosts and want to help them and earn their gratitude.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-04T14:44:35-08:00","id":1547521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh?p=1547517#post1547517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of my dislikes with Changeling: the Dreaming is that it is not always easy to connect a Kith with the kind of fairies they are supposed to emulate. Some are easy. Boggans relate to any kind of household or domestic fairies of which there are many examples. Nockers are obviously fairies who live underground, mine, and create magical items. Other kiths are either too specific or too vague. Sluagh are among the later.<br /> <br /> Yes, sluagh are obviously inspired by the Scottish fairy of the same name, but they are the host of the unforgiven dead which is not the same thing as the kith. Instead, the kith just seems to be a generic &quot;spooky fairy&quot; type. However, there don't seem to be many such fairies, and those that are scary often fit one of the other kiths better. Despite my attempts to read more fairy tales and folklore, I can't find any examples that make me go, &quot;Ah, that should be a sluagh!&quot; Instead, at best they just fill the role of general childhood boogeymen that are scary, but not very harmful.<br /> <br /> Folklore does have many examples of fairy type creatures whose purpose seems to be to keep children away from dangerous places until they become old enough to safely handle those places/situations. Or to stop being nuisances that bother their parents or other adults. But again, many actual specific fairies of this kind seem to fit other kiths better. So we're faced with the most generic type of nursery bogie. Still if we go down that route, we have another opportunity to use that theme to make them a kind of protectors of children whether they be kind neighbors, store owners of products that children like (maybe candy or toys), babysitters, governesses, school teachers and the like. Whether the sluagh kith is well designed for that is another issue.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> While they cannot control or threaten ghosts since they don't have powers, they do have something else they can do which does not require special powers to affect the dead. They can befriend ghosts and do favors for them. In a sense, they are just very good mediums that can do that &quot;job&quot; much better than most human mediums. So they can protect fetters, assist them in hunting Pathos, and do other favors for them in the Skinlands that they cannot do themselves. In exchange, the ghosts can do them equivalent favors like spying on people, using the occasional Arcanoi to accomplish something for them, and other favors.<br /> <br /> Of course, that means the ST needs to work a lot on developing who these ghosts are, what they want, and what they can do. Some STs are always up for that challenge, while for others it is a difficulty so they neglect it, and PCs of that kith can never make good use of their birthright. But if the ST is up for it, then the sluagh just need to be friendly and kind to ghosts and want to help them and earn their gratitude.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why not simply give them a third birth right? Allow the Sluagh to use their powers on wraiths or spectors as if they were commoner fae, and all other spirits as if they were fae nobles. <br /> <br /> This would make it highly advantageous for the Sluagh and Wraiths to work together. After all, each group can greatly aid or harm the other. Keeping together as allies would automatically be the safest path.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-04T15:29:44-08:00","id":1547523,"title":"","content":"Is there a need to let the use Fae instead of Actor to use Arts on wraiths? Just being able to perceive and speak to ghosts lets them use lots of Arts on the dead, and as I mentioned, Actor is already the Realm for Prodigals. Primal and Dragon's Ire might not be serious threats to ghosts because there isn't the inherent power to cause them physical harm, but I don't see anything that stops you from binding a ghost with Contract.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-04T19:37:12-08:00","id":1547529,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh?p=1547523#post1547523\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is there a need to let the use Fae instead of Actor to use Arts on wraiths? Just being able to perceive and speak to ghosts lets them use lots of Arts on the dead, and as I mentioned, Actor is already the Realm for Prodigals. Primal and Dragon's Ire might not be serious threats to ghosts because there isn't the inherent power to cause them physical harm, but I don't see anything that stops you from binding a ghost with Contract.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your points are valid. But a simple house rule that makes Sluagh better at dealing with ghosts seems useful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-04T20:08:49-08:00","id":1547531,"title":"","content":"How does making ghosts fall under the Fae Realm make them better at dealing with ghosts though?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-05T14:59:27-08:00","id":1547559,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh?p=1547531#post1547531\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How does making ghosts fall under the Fae Realm make them better at dealing with ghosts though?</div> </div> </div> </div>What would you do to make Sluagh better at dealing with and controlling ghosts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-05T16:57:50-08:00","id":1547563,"title":"","content":"I don't really see why they're not already extremely good at it? Having a Birthright that lets them target ghosts in the first place because they can actually sense the presence of the dead means they can use their Arts on wraiths freely where most changelings can't. Sluagh also have Prop as their favored Realm, which is a big boost for protecting stereotypical object-type Fetters.<br /> <br /> Since I don't see how the Sluagh aren't already good at this (and head-and-shoulders above the other Kiths), I don't see a particular need to make them better.<br /> <br /> Maybe, if you're using 2e instead of C20, and a lot of the better Arts to use with ghosts were locked away as Kith-exclusive, secret, or non-Art powers that C20 just made proper Arts, I could see a case for it, but in C20? I don't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-06T02:48:07-08:00","id":1547572,"title":"","content":"I did do a word search through the 20th anniversary rules and the word ghost does pop up a few times, there's even a couple of wraiths in Chapter nine under Prodigals.<br /> <br /> What is interesting is that ghosts arespecifically mentioned under the Fae Realm:<br /> <br /> **** Elusive Gallain<br /> Potent adepts of the Fae Realm may direct cantrips at those<br /> strange fae set apart from the Kithain by the inscrutable will of<br /> the Dreaming. This category generally includes the nunnehi and<br /> Menehune, Adhene, inanimae, Thallain, and any other bizarre<br /> denizen of the Dreaming. <i>It also includes any inexplicable yet<br /> bodiless beings that changelings occasionally encounter, such<br /> as spirits or ghosts.</i><br /> Possible examples: Goblins, kachinas, water babies, hana,<br /> <b>ghosts</b>, naraka, Hsien<br /> <br /> Which is interesting because under Actor it mentions that 'Prodigals such as Vampires and Werewolves' are included. So depending on your view either could work, although there seems to be a bias towards the Fae Realm.<br /> <br /> So as Heavy Arms mentioned before it's going to be realitvely easy for a Sluagh to affect ghosts with their Arts, even though there aren't any specific 'Necromantic' Arts. Which helps a lot with my view of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-06T04:19:30-08:00","id":1547576,"title":"","content":"The Spirit World (pp.419\u2013420) is a set of systems allowing Nunnehi and Menehune to interact with spirits and the spirit world. It consists of three parts: World Sight, Spirit Speech, and Spirit Walk. A Sluagh's Sharpened Senses already provides ghost-only versions of the first two; and Spirit Walk requires the traveler to have the Wayfare Art. So why not adapt that for the Sluagh? For example, say that a Sluagh with at least a dot in Legerdemain can couple it with the &quot;communicate with ghosts&quot; aspect of Sharpened Senses to physically interact with the Shadowlands kind of like how they interact with chimerical reality: for this Sluagh, nearby ghostly phenomena are effectively chimerical. This would let her use any Cantrips on them in the same way they can be used on chimerical beings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-07T12:07:06-08:00","id":1547620,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh?p=1547576#post1547576\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Spirit World (pp.419\u2013420) is a set of systems allowing Nunnehi and Menehune to interact with spirits and the spirit world. It consists of three parts: World Sight, Spirit Speech, and Spirit Walk. A Sluagh's Sharpened Senses already provides ghost-only versions of the first two; and Spirit Walk requires the traveler to have the Wayfare Art.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for pointing that out, I hadn't read that section yet and it's really fascinating - so they basically have 'Theurge' type abilities from Werewolf which makes for some interesting potential crossover material.<br /> <br /> A sluagh, with Legerdemain's Gimmix power, and the appropriate Actor/Fae (see above for interpretation of this) should definitely be able to affect the Shadowlands, because if you can see ghosts/Wraiths you should also be able to see their environs, at least partially.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-05T16:03:33-08:00","id":1548239,"title":"","content":"I'm inclined to agree with the others. If you have the ability to see and communicate with an entire parallel set of NPCs, with their own goals, society, and supernatural powers (with variable ability to affect your own reality), and you can't think of a way to leverage that, I don't know what to tell you.<br /> <br /> Sluagh are the social butterflies of the Changeling world. Just, you know, with ghosts. They can pull strings, gather info, or call in backup that no one else can. Such a PC is effective when they sit at the heart of a web of contacts, allies, debts, and obligations.<br /> <br /> I can also imagine Wraiths prefer dealing with Sluagh more than, say, the Giovanni. Not just because their aura isn't rancid, but precisely BECAUSE they can't necessarily control a Wraith directly. (Though I agree with Heavy Arms's point that their Arts should be able to target them, where appropriate). Clan Giovanni are the slavers of the Deadlands, colonial powers that shackle the dead and force them to work against their will. Whereas a Sluagh, like many spirit mediums, is a contact that can be reasoned with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-26T12:20:55-07:00","id":1549586,"title":"","content":"RE the Sluagh Birthrights, it's important to note that since you can see ghosts, you can target them by using the Fae Realm. Fae 4 lets you target all kinds of spirits, and since you know where they are, Sovereign + Fae 4 + being a Sluagh makes you a peerless Necromancer. With Wayfare or Dragon's Ire you could grant a ghost success in some scheme or battle, with Naming you could alter a ghost's nature, with Contract you could enter into binding deals with ghosts.<br /> <br /> Hell, worth remembering that there are Trods which lead to the shadowlands, and that Wayfare can get you into the realms of the restless dead. All this to say that the Sluagh Birthright is actually pretty amazing if you want to use Cantrips on ghosts, and there are plenty of reasons you'd want to do so.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-05T19:18:48-07:00","id":1549856,"title":"","content":"Changeling is also a game about creativity, and I think being &quot;generic spook&quot; actually does a favor to the Sluagh in that, while roles for them aren't that obvious, they can actually fill many of them.<br /> <br /> A Sluagh can fulfill any role you imagine once you put a &quot;spooky&quot; twist on it, and while this does mean that other kiths usually fulfill those roles &quot;better&quot;. the &quot;Sluagh twist&quot; makes for a greater diversity of takes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-06T02:24:51-07:00","id":1549865,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh?p=1549586#post1549586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">RE the Sluagh Birthrights, it's important to note that since you can see ghosts, you can target them by using the Fae Realm.</div> </div> </div> </div>One of those &quot;blink and you'll miss it&quot; rules. For those unaware, you'll find this in CtD on page 234; the last sentence for the four-dot &quot;Elusive Gallain&quot;: <br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\">It also includes any inexplicable yet bodiless beings that changelings occasionally encounter, such as spirits or ghosts.</div><br /> So yeah; combining that with a Sluagh's Sharpened Senses, we have a 100% published rules way for the Sluagh to apply their Arts to ghosts. And that opens up <i>\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200ball sorts </i>\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bof possibilities\u2026","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-06T04:23:36-07:00","id":1549867,"title":"","content":"Yep! Fae 4 is massively influential.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-13T14:45:15-07:00","id":1551009,"title":"","content":"Oh, worth noting that since Fae 4 covers the Wraiths of WTO and the Spirits of WTA, and so on, any random Changeling with a Fae of 2 can spend 2 Glamour to 'cheat' out a Fae 4 Cantrip.This makes Changelings very, very dangerous if they know what they're doing.<br /> <br /> For the Spirits of WTA, Form is Reality. A simple Metamorphosis 1 Cantrip can turn a salmon into a cod, so think of what it might do to a Salmon Totem. Changing a spirit's appearance changes what it is, their function follows their form. Similarly, Fae magic can easily blur the lines between a Wraith and their Shadow, or totally redefine a Fetter and its relationship with a ghost.<br /> <br /> To cut a long story short, Fae 4 can make you a very upsetting individual who challenges the core assertions of a whole 'faction' in the WoD. The humble &quot;Eidolon&quot; of a Technocrat might be hunted down in their dreams and turned into a frankly mystical Boogeyman, a creature which is hard to interpret as anything other than a mystical Avatar. Of course very few Changelings would entirely understand what they were doing, but if you're invading the dreams of an enemy you're going to start messing around with things that seem important to them, see what harms them most keenly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-14T12:22:32-07:00","id":1551020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh?p=1551009#post1551009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the Spirits of WTA, Form is Reality. A simple Metamorphosis 1 Cantrip can turn a salmon into a cod, so think of what it might do to a Salmon Totem. Changing a spirit's appearance changes what it is, their function follows their form.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is this actually true? There are spirits who have the Charm Shapeshift. It clearly states that it only changes the spirit's form, not its abilities of powers. So obviously there are spirits whose form is not reality. Are these spirits simply an exception? Or does it reveal the limitation of such &quot;spells&quot; when talking about spirits in general?<br /> <br /> Do such powers actually work across the Gauntlet? Or would it only apply if the spirit is materialized in the physical world, or if the changeling actually enters the Umbra or the spirit enters the Dreaming? Personally I would not allow such powers to work across the Gauntlet, but would be open when both parties are in the same &quot;dimension&quot;. Likewise for wraiths and the Shroud.<br /> <br /> Also, the Metamorphosis Art clearly says it can change a living being into another living being. Does that mean it cannot affect undead like vampires or wraiths even with the Actor Realm? Is the mention of the &quot;living&quot; incidental and should be taken as really meaning anything affected by the Actor realm? Or it an inherent limitation of the Art? Does it affect corpses? Or would Primal be the appropriate Art there? As ST, I might rule it can affect vampires because they are part of the &quot;living&quot; world and some semblance of &quot;life&quot;, but not wraiths because the Art itself does not affect them (this being a limitation of the Art, not an issue of the Realm used). Also, some of the individual arts of Metamorphosis seem to state it only affects the changeling casting the cantrip as opposed to also other living beings. Again, this may just be poor wording on behalf of the writer, or actually be an intended restriction.<br /> <br /> Because such powers are written in ways to communicate what it can do in typical situations, but not written to address crossover elements that are not typically included in that setting, I am often leery at simply applying RAW and not thinking things through. I might be willing to allow such powers affect Gafflings, but automatically fail for Jagglings and allow Jagglings to be able to &quot;flip&quot; Gafflings of its type back to what they are. Just say there's a new Charm for that and Jagglings always have it. Not to mention said changeling may now have lots of spirit notoriety. Or maybe not worry about it because such transformations are temporary anyway.<br /> <br /> Does Fae actually cover spirits? Wraiths are specifically mentioned in Actor, while Fae itself just mentions creatures of the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> Please note I do not have C20, so this may be something clarified or changed in that edition. Or some other sourcebook provided possible crossover rules.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-14T12:31:52-07:00","id":1551021,"title":"","content":"C20's Fae Realm specifies that it lets Cantrips target &quot;spirits&quot;, yeah, with the implication being that in the Changeling cosmology the Wraiths and Spirits of the WoD are composed of some exotic glamour one step away from that of the Dreaming. There are also Trods leading into the Middle and Low Umbra, and you can use Wayfare to cross into the Umbra, and also turning a regular Trod into a Trod leading to the Umbra is possible with some Cantrips.<br /> <br /> And yeah you're absolutely right about the inconsistency in Form = Reality, there's a line in the W20 Umbra book about how important a spirit's form is to it, that changing its shape changes its reality. Arguably a shapechanging Charm is just a simple 'disguise' instead of an actual transformation. Metamorphosis meanwhile, which in C20 can do stuff like turn a bowler hat into a stetson, for example, not just change living things, might be transforming, or it might be applying a 'glamour'.<br /> <br /> There is a viable crossover implication that a random Pooka could make an enemy for life by turning a Falcon Spirit into an Eagle Spirit (the difference is likely very significant to the spirit and its brood!), but it's also reasonable to expect that powerful spirits would be more resistant to such change and have defenses against it!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-03T01:33:10-07:00","id":1559196,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1547490-am-i-missing-something-about-the-sluagh?p=1547517#post1547517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Yes, sluagh are obviously inspired by the Scottish fairy of the same name, but they are the host of the unforgiven dead which is not the same thing as the kith. Instead, the kith just seems to be a generic &quot;spooky fairy&quot; type. However, there don't seem to be many such fairies, and those that are scary often fit one of the other kiths better. Despite my attempts to read more fairy tales and folklore, I can't find any examples that make me go, &quot;Ah, that should be a sluagh!&quot; Instead, at best they just fill the role of general childhood boogeymen that are scary, but not very harmful.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Sluagh remind me of (in concept) the witte wieven (white wives) of Dutch folklore, the kind of fae who straddle the line between fae and ghosts and witches, wraith-like figures who hide gold in their hills and marshes and forests and spirit people away to dance with them in the night.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28946-pryderi","name":"Pryderi","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}